            <ArticleTitle>Diet quality and markers of endothelial function: the CARDIA study.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>632-8</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.numecd.2013.12.010</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0939-4753(14)00003-9</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND AND AIM" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">Dietary patterns are associated cross-sectionally with cellular adhesion molecules (CAMs). We studied prospective associations of three dietary patterns with CAMs.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS AND RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">In the Coronary Artery Risk Development in Young Adults (CARDIA) study, diet was assessed at years 0 (1985-86) and 7 (1992-93) examinations. Four circulating CAMs (E-selectin, P-selectin, soluble intercellular adhesion molecule 1 (sICAM-1), and vascular cellular adhesion molecule (VCAM)) were assayed at years 7 and 15 (2000-01). We created one index score &quot;A Priori Diet Quality Score&quot; and derived dietary patterns using principal components analysis (PCA). Multivariable linear regression models predicted year 15 CAMs from averaged (year 0/7) dietary patterns. The A Priori Diet Quality Score rated 46 food groups beneficial, neutral or adverse based on hypothesized health effects. We derived two PCA dietary patterns: &quot;fruit and vegetables (FV)&quot; (high intakes of fruit, vegetables, and whole grains) and &quot;meat&quot; (high intakes of red meat, refined grain, and butter). All dietary patterns were related to E-selectin and sICAM-1. P-selectin was not related to the FV dietary pattern. VCAM was only related to the A Priori Diet Quality Score. Strongest associations were for the meat dietary pattern with E-selectin (effect size 28% of an SD (+3.9/13.7 ng/mL)) and P-selectin (effect size 37% of an SD (+4.1/11.2 ng/mL)) and the A Priori Diet Quality Score with sICAM-1 (effect size 34% of an SD (-15.1/44.7 ng/mL)) and VCAM (effect size of 26% of an SD (-45.1/170.3 ng/mL)).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">This prospective analysis suggests that dietary patterns are associated with CAMs.</AbstractText>
